    "result_text": "All 30 tests pass with **BUILD SUCCESS** and no regressions.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the daily-limit rule so that creating an owner is rejected with **429 Too Many Requests** once 100+ owners have already been registered today (by `registrationDate`).\n\nFollowing the existing `EnsureCityCapacity` pattern, I added four files and wired one step:\n\n1. **`EnsureDailyLimit.java`** (function) \u2014 counts owners whose `registrationDate` equals the server's current date via `OwnerRepository.findAll()`, throwing `DailyLimitException` when the count reaches 100.\n2. **`DailyLimitException.java`** \u2014 checked exception (extends `Exception`) so it surfaces in the `throws` clause.\n3. **`DailyLimitExceptionHandler.java`** \u2014 responds `429 TOO_MANY_REQUESTS` with a `ProblemDetail`, mirroring the other escalation handlers.\n4. **`org.springframework.samples.petclinic.rest.escalation.DailyLimitException.yml`** \u2014 maps the exception to its handler.\n5. **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 inserted an `ensureDailyLimit` step (with `govern: [ transaction ]`) between `ensureCityCapacity` and `build`.\n\nThe pre-existing `Cp19Tests` (which creates 100 owners then expects the 101st to be rejected with 429) now passes, and the full suite (30 tests) remains green.",
